## 2. Decisions taken

| Decision | Choice | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| Name | Git Graph Next | Chosen by the user over `Commit Graph` / `Gitline`. Carries a naming-proximity risk to the original, accepted knowingly. |
| Scope of v0.1 | Full parity with Git Graph + the fixes | Chosen by the user over a smaller first release. Months of work before publishing; the roadmap below is sequenced so parity is reached in usable stages. |
| Licence | MIT | Deliberately the opposite of the original's restriction. Forks of *this* project must stay legal. |
| Language / build | TypeScript, esbuild | One bundler for extension and webview. No framework in the webview: the commit table must stay fast with 100k+ rows. |
| Git access | Spawn the `git` binary | Full feature coverage. Wrapped behind `GitExecutor` so a future web build (#687) has a seam to swap. |
| Tests | `node:test`, esbuild-compiled | This Node build lacks type stripping (`ERR_NO_TYPESCRIPT`), so tests are bundled to `out/test/` first. |
| Graph layout location | Computed in the webview | `layoutGraph` is pure; the host sends commits + refs only, and the view can re-layout without a round trip. |
| Stash rows | One synthetic row per stash, parent = base commit only | A stash is really a 2–3 parent merge with an internal "index on…" commit; drawing that is noise. Inserted by date, never below its base. |
| Context menu | Own DOM menu in the webview | #873 / #863. No native webview menu contributions at all. |
| Activity Bar container | Own container "Git Graph Next" with two views: a compact webview graph and a native tree "Changes" | Requested by the user in session 3. The same webview bundle runs in both places (`data-mode` on `<body>`); one `GraphController` class drives either host. |
| Changes of a commit | Against the **first parent**; stash against its base; Uncommitted against HEAD incl. untracked | Matches what `git show` shows for a normal commit; merges say so explicitly in the details pane. |
| Diff documents | `git-graph-next:` URIs, read-only, path = repo-relative file (tab name + language), everything else in the JSON query | Uncommitted diffs use the real `file:` URI on the right, so the diff is editable. |
| Filtered graphs | Path filter: git rewrites parents (`--parents`). Author filter and `--follow`: **we** rewrite parents (`src/graph/rewrite.ts`) from a `rev-list --parents` walk capped at 50k commits | git leaves original parents for those, so every edge would dangle. Beyond the cap edges trail off rather than attach wrongly. |
| Author filter semantics | Literal, case-insensitive (`--fixed-strings --regexp-ignore-case`); several authors = OR | Users pick names from suggestions or the context menu; "John (Work)" must not be a regex. Note `--fixed-strings` also applies to `--grep` when both are used. |
| Search language | GitHub-style operators (`author:`, `committer:`, `message:`, `hash:`, `branch:`, `tag:`, `ref:`, `after:`, `before:`, `date:>…`), quoted phrases, all terms AND, literal and case-insensitive | Asked for in #147 itself. One shared matcher (`src/search/query.ts`) for loaded commits and history, so both agree on what a match is. |
| History search | git narrows to a *superset* of matches (necessary conditions only), then the shared matcher decides; position from the graph's own hash list (capped at 200k) | Exact semantics without reimplementing them as git arguments. The only risk is under-matching, so narrowing never uses anything that is not strictly necessary. Known gap: free-text words split across different fields (one in the message, one in a ref name) are not found in *unloaded* history. |
| Exclusions (#360) | Setting `excludeBranches` + per-repo patterns in the Branches dropdown + "Hide This Branch/Tag"; hidden refs' labels come from asking git (`rev-parse` with the same globs) | Label hiding follows git's own matching rules exactly instead of a re-implementation. |
| Extra log args (#591) | Setting `extraLogArguments` + per-repo text field in the Filter bar, shell-style quoting, no expansion; a deny-list with reasons (format/diff/count/`--reverse`/`-g`/`--output`/external programs/colour/`--`), enforced on the host | Everything else is allowed. Any extra args turn on parent rewriting (e.g. `--no-merges` drops commits git does not reconnect). |
| Pinned branches (#207) | Setting `graph.pinnedBranches` (globs, priority order) + per-repo "Pin to Own Column" from a label; default none | Pinned lanes take the leftmost columns and the first colours, so they are also stably coloured. Only branches whose tip is loaded are pinned. |
| Revision documents (#598) | A read-only `FileSystemProvider` for `git-graph-next:` instead of a text content provider; the missing side of a `.ipynb` diff is an empty but valid notebook | VS Code opens notebooks and other custom editors only from a file system. Also byte-exact for binaries. |
| Git notes (#475) | `git notes list` marks noted commits (badge); the text is read with `git notes show` only when a commit is opened | Note blobs never enter the NUL-delimited log output, so they cannot corrupt parsing. |
| Issue links (#313) | Auto-detected GitHub (`#n`) / GitLab (`#n`, `!n`) rules from the origin remote + configurable rules with `$1…` and `${host}/${owner}/${repo}/${repoUrl}`; host opens http(s) only | One global rule can serve every repo (asked for in the issue's comments). |
| Branch colours (#254) | `graph.branchColours` (pattern → colour, fixed along first-parent history) + opt-in `graph.colourCommitRows` stripe | Default look unchanged unless enabled. |
| Compact mode (#387) | Fold runs of ≥3 adjacent commits with one parent, one child, no label, not HEAD / match / selected into a stand-in row that keeps the first hash (so no edge rewriting); click expands | Shows the branch structure but keeps the hidden commit count visible. Adjacent-row runs only: in date order an interleaved chain folds less than in topological order. |
| Write actions (Phase 3) | Typed `GitAction` union → `validateAction` (nothing may start with `-`, hashes are 40-hex, stashes `stash@{n}`) → `git check-ref-format` for new names → `planAction` (exact argv, `--` after revisions) → `ActionRunner` (per-repo queue, progress, refresh) | The webview builds actions but is not trusted to have built them correctly. |
| Action UI | Own dialogs in the webview; errors stay in the dialog | A mistyped name can be fixed in place. Destructive actions are marked (warning border, red button). |
| Credentials | Prompts stay disabled; on a credential failure the host offers "Run in Terminal" with the same command (shell-quoted) | A hidden prompt would hang forever; the terminal can prompt. |
| Webview testing | `test/webview.smoke.test.ts` boots the real webview in jsdom and drives it with host messages | Added in session 8: the UI had grown too large to rely on the type checker and live checks alone. |
| Interactive rebase (#757, #113) | git's own editor protocol: `GIT_SEQUENCE_EDITOR`/`GIT_EDITOR` = a bundled script (`dist/editor.js`) run with VS Code's Node (`ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE`), talking to the extension over a local socket with a per-session token; the file opens in a normal VS Code editor with Start/Cancel; closing the tab = done | git builds the list itself, so every rebase option works; no reimplementation of the todo format. |
| Squash / drop selected commits (#182) | The same bridge rewrites git's own todo lines (`rewriteTodo`), optionally shown for review; refused off the current branch, over merges, or with a stale base (`checkRewrite`) | Only the lines for the chosen commits change. |
| Patches (#538) | Paths come only from VS Code's file dialogs on the host, never from the view; `format-patch` per commit, `git diff --binary HEAD` for uncommitted changes; apply with `git apply` or `git am` | Nothing from the webview becomes a path. |
| Code review (session 11) | Review a commit, two commits, or a branch against another from their merge base (the pull-request view). Files snapshot at start; opening a diff anywhere marks it reviewed; Alt+] / Alt+[ (context key `gitGraphNext.reviewActive`) step through unreviewed files from the diff editor; sessions in `workspaceState` | The user asked for review to be "fast and easy to compare". Keyboard stepping from the editor avoids going back to the graph per file. |
| All Changes | `vscode.changes` (multi-diff editor, VS Code 1.86+), feature-detected at run time; falls back to the first file's diff | Keeps `engines` at 1.85. |
| Webview → host values | Every `ChangeTarget` is validated at the controller boundary (`src/view/validation.ts`); branch names for review must not look like options | Found in session 11: read paths (selectCommit, openDiff) had never validated hashes, only the actions did. |
| Avatars | Opt-in (`fetchAvatars`, off); fetched on the host for rows on screen; Gravatar by MD5 of the address, GitHub for no-reply addresses; 500-entry cache | Privacy: it tells third parties which authors appear. README's Privacy section says so. |
| Filter scope | Per repository, persisted in the panel's webview state; the sidebar never filters | Keeps the sidebar a quick overview. |
| Unimplemented commands | Removed from `package.json` until built | Contributed-but-unregistered commands fail with "command not found". To re-add: `viewLineHistory`, `clearAvatarCache`, `resumeWorkspaceCodeReview`, `endAllWorkspaceCodeReviews`, `openDiffOfCommit`, and their `editor/title`, `editor/context`, `explorer/context` (file) menu entries. |
| Dev loop | `npm run package && npx vsce package --no-dependencies && code --install-extension git-graph-next-0.1.0.vsix --force`, then *Developer: Reload Window* | The user verifies each step live in their own VS Code. The user commits; Claude does not. |

### Deferred / not yet decided

- **Marketplace publisher identity** (`publisher` in `package.json` is a placeholder). Needs the user: an Azure DevOps organisation, a publisher id, a `VSCE_PAT` secret; optionally an Open VSX namespace and `OVSX_PAT`.
- **Repository URL** (`repository` in `package.json` is a placeholder). vsce rewrites the README's relative image links to it, so screenshots on the Marketplace stay broken until it points at the real public repository.
- Whether to support `vscode.dev` (#687). The `GitExecutor` seam exists; nothing depends on the answer yet.

